1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:03,400
Liverpool v Man City, the last 20 minutes. Liverpool's win chance peaks at 91%.

2
00:00:03,600 --> 00:00:06,900
Then City score three. Here is what the broadcast could have shown.

3
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:11,400
Every Liverpool option, priced live: the goal chance if the ball goes to him, ranked.

4
00:00:11,700 --> 00:00:14,900
City's last line, tracked as the play moves.

5
00:00:15,100 --> 00:00:17,400
At the pass the furthest attacker is level: inside the 0.3 m tracking error.

6
00:00:17,700 --> 00:00:23,900
Then it freezes the real frame, reads the players and replays the moment 200 times.

7
00:00:24,100 --> 00:00:27,400
Szoboszlai shot. The pass to Salah was worth 2 more points of win chance.

8
00:00:27,700 --> 00:00:33,300
Every line is drawn on the real pitch, through the broadcast camera.

9
00:00:33,500 --> 00:00:38,400
Eze shot. Going back to Saka was the better ball.

10
00:00:38,700 --> 00:00:48,400
And it says when the player got it right: the pass into the box was no better.

11
00:00:48,700 --> 00:00:52,600
A refereeing call, explained from the tracking: the run, the contact, the closing speed.

12
00:00:52,800 --> 00:00:56,700
An assessment, not a ruling: about 15 km/h into the challenge, outside the box.

13
00:00:57,100 --> 00:01:06,400
The same intelligence becomes an analyst's breakdown, a casual fan's line, and a player's story.

14
00:01:06,900 --> 00:01:12,400
Measured on this footage: 99.3% of players found, graphics within 1.5 px of the painted lines.

